### A.1 The five supervisor-level questions

Separately from the decision records above, these five questions are the ones that need a supervisor's answer rather than the group's. They are drawn from the parallel gap analysis of the existing proposal and are recorded here so that the outline can be signed off in one meeting.

| # | Question | Why it is a supervisor's question | What this outline recommends | Where |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **SQ-1** | **Must the Cisco layer be genuinely manageable by the AI layer, or is a documented simulator gap acceptable?** | **This is the single most important question in the project.** If the answer is "genuinely manageable", Packet Tracer must be replaced or supplemented by a reachable Cisco image — which raises licensing, host-resource and reproducibility questions that the current frozen design forbids by client constraint. If the answer is "a documented gap is acceptable", the AI layer covers two families and the fifth brief line weakens | **Genuinely manageable**, because otherwise the project's stated purpose (AI managing the network) is undemonstrable for the Cisco family. Primary **CML**, fallback **hybrid**; if neither is permitted, take the **Section C.5** scope reduction and write it down | A, C.4, C.5, J-D-01 |
| **SQ-2** | **Where may the local model run, and on what hardware?** | The answer determines the model class, which determines the achievable use cases. The previous design's compute budget sized the AI host at **4 vCPU / 8 GB with no GPU**, which **cannot host a useful local model** — so the compute plan must be re-planned, not inherited | **School/lab machines, specification captured in week 1 (B1)**; the model **not** co-located with the 19-VM estate host; target tier **T2 (12–16 GB VRAM)** with a documented no-GPU fallback | E.1, J (B1/D-03/D-04) |
| **SQ-3** | **How far does "manages" go?** | The student has already chosen **read-only plus approved changes**. This **replaces** the previous design's deliberate exclusion of *any* state-changing action, and that change of position carries verification and academic-integrity consequences that need the supervisor's agreement | **Read-only by default; changes only through the six-field proposal → human approval → single-use token → execution → verification → audit workflow.** It is a strength, not a compromise | E.4, E.5, E.8, I.8 |
| **SQ-4** | **Is the AI/MCP layer now the assessed differentiator?** | If yes, the previous framing must be **formally withdrawn** — the corpus currently calls the AI line *"the weakest line"*, treats MCP as cuttable, and places local model serving out of scope. Those statements cannot stand alongside a project whose purpose is AI network management | **Yes.** MCP + local model is the differentiator; the framing change is listed in Section L.4 as an explicit withdrawal | L.4 |
| **SQ-5** | **What is the expected submission size and shape now?** | The existing corpus is **286,932 words**, which the gap analysis judges to be two to three times what this project should carry, with 23% of it in one project-management chapter | **Recommendation to confirm, not a unilateral decision:** a focus-corrected full proposal of **90,000–110,000 words** (hard ceiling 120,000 — a 58–69% cut), with the **AI / local-model + MCP line growing to about 18,000 words** and the implementation-schedule and project-management chapters shrinking hard (for example 66,754 → 15,000–18,000). **This outline itself is sized to 6,000–10,000 words** (see Section H.3) | H.3, L.4 |

