## C. Target architecture

### C.1 The three device families

| Family | Platform | Count (target) | Management interfaces that the AI plane may use |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Windows** | Windows Server guests (members of one AD DS domain) | 8–10 | WinRM (5985/5986), PowerShell Remoting, RPC-based AD/FSRM cmdlets, SNMPv3 agent, Windows Event Log, `windows_exporter` (9182), syslog via agent |
| **Linux** | Ubuntu Server guests | 9–10 | SSH (22), `systemctl`/`journalctl`, nftables, `node_exporter` (9100), rsyslog forward (514/udp) |
| **Cisco** | **Virtual IOS-XE/IOS devices with real management planes** (see Section C.4) | 5–8 | **SSH (22) for `show` commands**, **SNMPv3 (161/162)**, **RESTCONF (443) where the image supports it**, syslog (514), NetFlow if licensed, NTP for clock integrity |
| **AI plane** | One Ubuntu host (model serving + MCP servers) and one agent host, or one host with separated service identities | 2 | — (it is the consumer, not the managed element) |

### C.2 How they interconnect

- The **VM estate's segmentation** is delivered by hypervisor host-only networks, one per zone, DHCP disabled on every one of them (carried over from the frozen baseline: VLAN 20 management `10.20.20.0/24`, VLAN 30 server `10.10.30.0/24`, VLAN 50 egress transit `10.10.50.0/28`, VLAN 90 DMZ `10.10.90.0/24`, VLAN 80 storage `10.10.80.0/24`). This is an **emulated** segmentation: the outcome is real, the mechanism is not 802.1Q.
- The **Cisco layer** provides the routed core, the WAN links, the branch sites, and the perimeter. It connects to the VM estate through one or more layer-3 handoff segments (the baseline's `TRANSIT-EGRESS` and `LAN-HQ-CORE` concepts are reused for this purpose).
- **Cross-family traffic** flows routed, never bridged. `172.16.0.0/16` and `192.168.0.0/16` remain unallocated, and `.local`/`.lan`/`contoso`/`example.com` remain banned strings.
- **The AI plane sits in the management zone (VLAN 20 / `Z-MGMT`)**, not in the server zone, and every one of its outbound connections is an allow-listed management protocol to a named host. It has no route to the isolated lab segment.

### C.2a THE REACHABILITY RULE — stated once, unambiguously

> ## No MCP tool, AI agent, Ansible playbook or script in this project may claim to reach, read from, or configure a Cisco Packet Tracer device.
>
> **Packet Tracer devices are NOT REACHABLE.** Packet Tracer exposes **no SSH server reachable by external tooling, no SNMP agent a real poller can query, no NETCONF endpoint and no RESTCONF endpoint, and no management API whatsoever.** Any sentence, table row, diagram arrow, tool description or test case that implies otherwise is a fabrication and must be deleted.

**Why this is stated here, at the top, rather than buried in a limitations chapter.** The existing proposal corpus contains a **real internal contradiction** on exactly this point, and it must be resolved by this outline rather than inherited:

| Where | What it says | Verdict |
|---|---|---|
| `sections\06-automation.md` §10.7 | *"Packet Tracer has no API, no SSH server reachable from real tooling, and no Ansible collection. No playbook, script or tool in this project can log in to a Packet Tracer device. Any statement to the contrary would be fabrication."* | **CORRECT — keep this, and lift the same discipline into the AI chapter** |
| `sections\07-ai.md` §11.6.3 | Defines a tool `get_interface_stats` as an *"SNMPv3 authPriv poll of the Packet Tracer device estate"* | **WRONG — withdraw.** The AI chapter contradicts the automation chapter |
| `sections\07-ai.md` §11.6.4; baseline §12.5 | A data-flow diagram feeding the agent from the Packet Tracer devices | **WRONG — withdraw.** Redraw so that no arrow points at a Packet Tracer device |
| `sections\07-ai.md` §11.7.2 | A literal `snmpwalk … <validated-device-ip>` against the device estate | **WRONG — withdraw**, unless the target is a genuinely reachable device |
| `sections\05-cisco.md` §9.2.4 | Packet Tracer SNMPv3 is *"unreliable"* and may be evidenced *"only on the server estate"* | **CORRECT and decisive** — the Cisco chapter's own support table already concedes the point that the AI chapter ignored |

**The rule that follows, and that governs every later writer:** *an interface exists for this project only if a command run from the management host, in this lab, returned data from that specific device, and the transcript is filed as evidence.* Anything else is a design intention and must be labelled as one.

### C.2b Device-reachability matrix (normative)

Every managed device in the estate appears here with **the concrete interface that makes it reachable**. This matrix replaces the old design's service-only port matrix as the authority for what the AI layer can touch. A device that is not in this matrix is not a managed device, and **no tool may be written against it**.

`REACHABLE` = a real interface exists and a transcript proves it. `NOT REACHABLE` = no interface exists in this project; nothing may claim to reach it.

| # | Device / family | Platform | **Status** | Reachability interface (the concrete mechanism) | What the AI layer may therefore do |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| RW-01 | `vpnz-dc01`, `vpnz-dc02` | Windows Server VM | **REACHABLE** | **WinRM over HTTPS 5986** (PowerShell Remoting, `svc_aiagent`); `windows_exporter` 9182 scrape; Event Log via WinRM; syslog agent 514/udp | Read service state, event IDs, AD objects, replication health. **Read only — never a write (N4)** |
| RW-02 | `vpnz-fs01` | Windows Server VM | **REACHABLE** | As RW-01, plus **RPC-based FSRM cmdlets** (`Get-FsrmQuota`, `Get-FsrmFileScreen`) over WinRM | Read quota and file-screen configuration; read disk usage |
| RW-03 | `vpnz-san01`, `vpnz-rras01`, and every other Windows member server | Windows Server VM | **REACHABLE** | As RW-01 | Read service, event and disk state |
| RW-04 | `vpnz-mon01` (metrics, logs, Git, Ansible) | Ubuntu Server VM | **REACHABLE** | **HTTPS API** (Prometheus 9090, Loki, Grafana 3000 read), **Git over SSH**, local filesystem read for the config archive | Query metrics, logs, alerts, inventory, drift baselines, change history |
| RW-05 | `vpnz-nft01`, `vpnz-nft02` | Ubuntu Server VM | **REACHABLE** | **SSH 22** with a forced-command read-only allow-list (`nft list ruleset`, `ip -s link`, `systemctl show`, `journalctl`); `node_exporter` 9100; rsyslog 514/udp | Read the ruleset, interface counters, service state and the **drop log** — the richest AI signal in the estate |
| RW-06 | `vpnz-dmzrev01/02`, `vpnz-lnxra01`, and every other Ubuntu guest | Ubuntu Server VM | **REACHABLE** | As RW-05 | Read service state, logs, disk, config files (allow-listed, secrets redacted) |
| RW-07 | Cisco devices — **CML or physical or DevNet** (per D-01) | Genuine IOS-XE / IOS | **REACHABLE** *(conditional on D-01/D-12)* | **SSH 22** (`show`-only allow-list, no config mode); **SNMPv3 authPriv 161/162**; **RESTCONF over HTTPS 443** where the image supports it; syslog 514/udp; NetFlow if licensed | Read routing, switching, ACL, interface, crypto and log state; read YANG-modelled configuration. **Never enter configuration mode (N5)** |
| RW-08 | `VP-HQ-FW-01` (ASA-class perimeter) | Genuine ASA / ASAv | **REACHABLE** *(conditional on D-01)* | **SSH**, **SNMPv3**, syslog; `show running-config`, `show access-list`, `show crypto ipsec sa` | Read ACL/NAT/crypto state and the deny log |
| RW-09 | `vpnz-ai01` / the agent host | Ubuntu VM | **REACHABLE** (self) | Local process control only | Its own audit log and health. **It is not a managed network element** |
| **NR-01** | **All Packet Tracer devices** — `VP-HQ-CORE-01/02`, `VP-HQ-DSW-A01..A04`, `VP-HQ-EDGE-01`, `VP-HQ-FW-01`, `VP-BR-*`, `VP-MO-*`, `VP-WC-*`, `VP-ISP-EDGE-01`, `VP-HQ-VOICE-01` | **Cisco Packet Tracer (simulator)** | **NOT REACHABLE** | **None. No SSH, no SNMP, no NETCONF, no RESTCONF, no API.** | **Nothing.** No MCP tool, no agent, no playbook, no script, no test case. Packet Tracer may appear only as a **design-time** artefact, drawn with a crossed-out arrow and labelled *"no reachable management interface"* |
| NR-02 | The hypervisor host's management plane | VMware/VirtualBox | **NOT REACHABLE** from the AI layer | Deliberately excluded | Nothing. Kept out of the blast radius by design |
| NR-03 | The isolated lab segment (`10.70.0.0/16`) | — | **NOT REACHABLE** | No route | Nothing. It is the designated source for deliberate-misuse tests only |

**Verification obligation that goes with this matrix.** For **every** `REACHABLE` row, a transcript is filed showing the interface returning data from that device (for example a `Test-WSMan` result, an `snmpwalk -v3` result, a `show version` result, a Prometheus target marked `up`). **A `REACHABLE` row with no transcript is a claim, not a fact, and the report must list it as unverified.** This is the same evidence discipline the automation chapter already applies to Ansible, applied to the AI layer.

**The matrix is a deliverable.** It becomes a table in the design chapter, a test family in the verification chapter (`TC-REACH-…`), and — critically — it is what makes `MCP-S-CISCO` either a real server or an honest omission. If RW-07 and RW-08 are not reachable, then `MCP-S-CISCO` **cannot exist**, and the scope reduction in Section C.5 applies.

### C.3 Where the AI management plane sits and what it touches

```
[FIGURE: Four-band architecture diagram, read top to bottom.

BAND 1 — "OPERATOR / HUMAN" (top). A single laptop box labelled "Operator
workstation (student)". One arrow leaves it downward labelled
"natural-language question / approval decision". Nothing else leaves this band.

BAND 2 — "AI MANAGEMENT PLANE (Z-MGMT, VLAN 20, 10.20.20.0/24)". Two boxes
side by side inside a dashed boundary labelled "no write path to the estate
except through the approval gate".
  Box A: "vpnz-ai01 — Agent runtime (agent loop, policy engine, approval gate,
          audit log /var/log/fyp/agent.jsonl)".
  Box B: "vpnz-llm01 — Local model server (Ollama or llama.cpp; OpenAI-compatible
          /v1 endpoint bound to 127.0.0.1 or the Z-MGMT address only)".
  An arrow runs A -> B labelled "localhost/private HTTP, tool-calling JSON".
  A separate small box below-right: "MCP server bundle (5 servers, per-domain
  service accounts): MCP-S-LINUX, MCP-S-WIN, MCP-S-CISCO, MCP-S-MON,
  MCP-S-DOC". Arrow A -> bundle labelled "MCP (stdio or streamable HTTP)".
  Arrow Operator -> A labelled "question / approve-deny".

BAND 3 — "MANAGED ESTATE (the network built FIRST)". Three columns, each with
its own arrow set coming down from the MCP bundle:
  Left column "Windows family (VLAN 30; 8-10 guests: vpnz-dc01, vpnz-dc02,
    vpnz-fs01, vpnz-san01, vpnz-rras01, ...)":
      arrow labelled "WinRM 5986 / PowerShell Remoting (read-only acct)"
      arrow labelled "SNMPv3 161 authPriv"
      arrow labelled "windows_exporter 9182 (scrape)"
      arrow labelled "syslog + Event Log forwarding 514/udp"
  Middle column "Linux family (VLAN 30 / 50 / 90; 9-10 guests: vpnz-mon01,
    vpnz-nft01/02, vpnz-dmzrev01/02, vpnz-lnxra01, ...)":
      arrow labelled "SSH 22 (read-only allow-listed commands: systemctl show,
        journalctl, nft list, ip -s link, df)"
      arrow labelled "node_exporter 9100 (scrape)"
      arrow labelled "rsyslog forward 514/udp"
  Right column "Cisco family (routed core, WAN edge, ASA-class perimeter; 5-8
    devices VP-HQ-CORE-01/02, VP-HQ-EDGE-01, VP-HQ-FW-01, branches)":
      arrow labelled "SSH 22 -> show commands only (allow-list)"
      arrow labelled "SNMPv3 161 authPriv poll + traps 162"
      arrow labelled "RESTCONF over HTTPS 443 (read-only GET of YANG paths)"
      arrow labelled "syslog 514/udp; NetFlow export if available"
  A caption note at the bottom of this band: "No arrow points from Band 2 into
  any device's configuration path. Every arrow is a read or a scrape."

BAND 4 — "DATA / OBSERVABILITY PLANE (feeds Band 2)". Inside one boundary:
  "vpnz-mon01: Prometheus (:9090) + Grafana (:3000) + Loki/syslog archive +
  Git repository + Ansible control node + configuration archive (/srv/tftp)".
  Arrows go UP from this band into the MCP-S-MON and MCP-S-DOC boxes in Band 2,
  labelled "PromQL / LogQL / alert API / inventory read".

Alongside Band 3 on the same level, a separate small boundary labelled
"DESIGN-TIME ONLY" containing "Cisco Packet Tracer project file (topology,
CCNA-level VLAN/trunk/RSTP design)". A dashed, CROSSED-OUT arrow runs from
Band 2 to this box with the label "NOT REACHABLE — Packet Tracer exposes no SSH,
SNMP or API surface; no automation reaches it". This crossed-out arrow is
deliberate and must be drawn.] Caption: Figure 1 — Where the AI management plane
sits. It reads from the estate and writes only to its own audit log; the one
dashed, crossed-out arrow marks the interface that does not exist and must never
