## M. Immediate next steps (next 1–2 weeks)

Ordered. **Items 1–4 are the three blocking items (B1, B2, B3) plus their licence gate.** Nothing else should start until they are answered.

1. **Record the lab machine's specification — BLOCKING (B1).** One page, in the decision log: **GPU model, exact VRAM in GB, system RAM, free disk, CPU and cores, OS and version, whether the machine is shared/bookable/dedicated for the 12 weeks, what the lab permits you to install, and whether outbound network access is permitted and to where.** Owner: S4, by end of week 1. **The model choice is blocked on this; do not select a model before it exists.**
2. **Ratify the Cisco platform — BLOCKING (B2).** Take the primary recommendation (**CML**, smallest viable topology) and the fallback (**hybrid: Packet Tracer for design + 2–4 genuinely reachable devices**) to the supervisor and get a decision recorded, **including a decision on the simulator-only case** and its Section C.5 scope reduction. Owner: S1, end of week 1.
3. **Check the CML licence, image entitlement and topology RAM cost the same day (D-12).** Confirm at the vendor's own product and documentation pages — **never from memory, a forum post or a mirror** — and record the tier, the images included and the terms relied on. If this fails, move to the fallback immediately rather than at week 6.
4. **Confirm what the school's rules permit** — a licensed platform, personal physical equipment, and a hosted sandbox are three different permissions and may be granted differently. Ask all three questions now; discovering a prohibition in week 6 costs the AI demonstration.
5. **Stand up the repository and the evidence scaffolding in parallel with 1–4**: the folder tree, the Git repository, the figure-caption template and the filename convention. Freeze the evidence standard now, while there are zero screenshots to re-caption.
6. **Answer D-05 (Windows vs Cisco depth), D-06 (committed use cases) and D-13 (offline vs hybrid), and print the RACI from Section G** so that all four members know what they own before week 2 begins.
7. **Prove one Cisco device is reachable as the week-3 gate, on a single device.** Do not design the topology first. A single device with a working SSH login and one successful `snmpwalk -v3`, with `show version` captured, de-risks more of this project than any amount of design work.
8. **Write the permission policy into the proposal verbatim**, with the six-field change proposal, the single-use token, the must-never list and the audit-log requirement — and **build test PB-2 (the refusal test) at the same time as the first mutating tool**, not after it. A gate added later is a gate that was not there when it mattered.
9. **Book the local-model spike (weeks 5–6) on the calendar now**, with an explicit output: *a decision, a measured malformed-tool-call rate, and a named model.* An open-ended spike becomes an open-ended project.
10. **Start the device-reachability matrix (Section C.2b) on day 1, and file a transcript for every row you can.** This is the cheapest possible de-risking: run `Test-WSMan` against one Windows guest, `ssh` one Linux guest, `snmpwalk -v3` one Cisco device, and check one Prometheus target — then write down what answered. **Every row you cannot evidence is a row the AI layer cannot use**, and knowing that in week 1 rather than week 9 is the whole point.
11. **Draft the MUST-only service list from Section D.2 as a one-page checklist** and put it on the wall. It is the contract for what "the network is built" means, and it is what gate G2 will be judged against.
12. **Find and delete every Packet Tracer automation claim in the existing corpus.** Do this before any new writing: `07-ai.md` §11.6.3, §11.6.4 and §11.7.2 contradict `06-automation.md` §10.7, and a reader who finds one contradiction will doubt the whole document. The change record in Section L.4 is the checklist.

